The fix for the off-by-one error in your boundary condition should be:

```javascript
for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i)  {
    dp[i] = a[i];
    for (int j = i - 1; j >= 1; --j) {
        if (a[i] % a[j] == 0) {
            dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + a[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

Explanation:

  * The error is in the `i` loop. It should run `i` from `1` to `n`, but in the code, it runs `i` from `1` to `n` inclusive. By changing the last part of the `++i` to `--i` before the for loop, it will properly iterate from `1` to `n - 1` only.

